<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Durrani Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1823 CE · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The empire founded by Ahmad Shah Durrani in 1747, regarded as the foundation of the modern state of Afghanistan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> assassination in 1747, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Khan Abdali</a>, commander of his Afghan bodyguard, was chosen as king by a tribal assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. Taking the title Durr-i Durran, 'Pearl of Pearls', he founded the Durrani dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai clan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, and in 1761 crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a>. Revenue came from Indian plunder and tribute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribal chiefs held great power, and the state relied on their loyalty. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> Shah moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> to Kabul in 1776.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur's</a> sons, rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Shah Zaman</a>, Shah Mahmud and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-durrani">Shah Shuja</a>, and the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> ministers, broke the empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and by 1823 the Barakzais held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozais</a> kept the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-herat">Principality of Herat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/durrani-empire.jpg" alt="Durrani Empire" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: AfghanTsakhtan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Durrani_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1823 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad Shah Durrani, Timur Shah Durrani, Shah Shuja Durrani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sadozai Kingdom, Afghan Empire, Durrani dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1785&amp;focus=durrani-empire" aria-label="Durrani Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6437 927 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Durrani Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6757 1183L6298 1144M6757 1183L6918 1285M6757 1183L6757 1183M6757 1183L6582 1190M6757 1183L6695 1282M6757 1183L6780 1465M6757 1183L6811 1500M6757 1183L6931 1282M6757 1183L6738 1372M6757 1183L6867 1359M6757 1183L6986 1319"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6918 1285h0M6757 1183h0M6582 1190h0M6695 1282h0M6780 1465h0M6811 1500h0M6931 1282h0M6738 1372h0M6867 1359h0M6986 1319h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kabul. <a href="/atlas?year=1785&amp;focus=durrani-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
